This is a temporary way to handle files/features from linux that we need.

Files taken from riscv-linux:
arch/riscv/lib/uaccess.S -> uaccess.S
arch/riscv/include/asm/asm.h-> asm/asm.h
arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h-> asm/csr.h
arch/riscv/include/asm/linkage.h -> asm/linkage.h
include/uapi/linux/const.h -> linux/const.h
include/linux/linkage.h -> linux/linkage.h

Files containing modified linux bits:
partial_linkage.h
uaccess.h

================================ LICENSE ================================

The files listed above are taken from the Linux kernel and are licensed
GPL-2.0-only. linux/const.h carries GPL-2.0 WITH Linux-syscall-note.

uaccess.S is compiled into the rt_tmplib library and linked into the Eyrie
runtime, and asm/csr.h is included by runtime/sys/ and runtime/loader-binary/.
The effective license of a built Eyrie binary is therefore GPL-2.0-only, NOT
the BSD-3-Clause of runtime/LICENSE.

BSD-3-Clause is GPL-compatible, so the combination is permitted; what it is
not is BSD-licensed. Anyone redistributing a built Eyrie binary must comply
with GPL-2.0, including making the corresponding source available.

This directory is NOT covered by runtime/LICENSE. See NOTICE section 4 at the
top of the repository.

This arrangement is inherited from upstream Keystone. It does not affect sdk/
or examples/, which are separate programs and remain BSD-3-Clause.
